> Verification and references

Native tests exhaust all4096 three-vertex directed-graph/root-set combinations
against independent transitive closure, verify every stable map/order/field/root
output and actual phase record, and retain the original even-id chain. Shared
liveness tests inspect real root/pop/reference/stack transitions, duplicates,
self references, cycles, complete invalid unreachable fields, exact quotas,
ignored sizes, ownership and a100000-object iterative chain. Existing
memory/format regression suites remain. The ten-report batch passes 95 CTest
cases, 30 directly instrumented ASan/UBSan cases, 121 compiled and executed
public examples, 601/601 scoped documentation definitions and 11 representative
hovers. Independent integer, Fraction, graph, string and NumPy references verify
82,729 records, including the complete original seeded LSH study. Frozen
comparisons preserve ordinary legacy results; malformed-input,
sentinel-collision, ownership and explicit-budget corrections are documented
above. Thirteen checkout headers and thirteen installed headers compile
independently, and an installed-header native consumer links and runs. Each
complete report program runs against the checkout library; all four native
figures are reviewed and HTML/Markdown exports preserve computed results and
image bytes. Batch receipts: `build/mepml-conversion/batch-214-223/`.

F. L. Morris(1978),
[A Time- and Space-Efficient Garbage Compaction Algorithm|https://doi.org/10.1145/359576.359583],
Communications of the ACM21(8),662–665, studies physical garbage compaction.
This report illustrates liveness, forwarding and consistent reference
translation using an explicitly allocating unit-slot graph model; it does not
claim to implement Morris’s physical compaction algorithm.
